I must say, I agree with the person who suggested improving the interface.
It can be quite a hassle at times to have to constantly keep hitting the
space bar to skip through all the --more-- statements.  I've played Moria
and NetHack, and I must say, I enjoy NetHack more.  However, Moria has been 
have been laid out with extreme care and instead of constantly putting it
down, maybe we can get something out of it.  After all, everybody who reads/
posts to this net spends a pretty considerable amount of time playing the
game - i.e. using the interface. 

Take the 'x' key in Moria, it lets you look in a direction and see
what is in a given direction. If you want to look at that quivering blob
across the room, you type 'x' and the direction.  This is very fast and
efficient, especially since it is used a great deal.  The / key in NetHack
also serves this purpose, but is much more awkward.  You must first answer
a question about the interface, and then you have to move the cursor onto
the character that you want to identify.  Naturally, this has it's advantages
if you don't want to identify something that is on a straight line from your
character, but more often than not it is a monster in the room that you want
to check out, or an item across the room.  

The game is exceptional (just look at it's following!) but the interface
could use some lessons from other games that have been around longer.  This
is no insult - it's merely that the people who wrote those other (lesser)
games made all the mistakes for us...why not learn from them!

Cheers,
  -Bharat

